probably the most
followers on Twitter.

00:26:35.760 --> 00:26:38.229
He was the president
of the United States.

00:26:38.229 --> 00:26:41.132 align:start size:88% position:13%
Our very first amendment
is freedom of speech,

00:26:41.132 --> 00:26:43.001
and they took them off.

00:26:43.001 --> 00:26:45.403 align:start size:88% position:13%
And the place I heard
the most complaints from

00:26:45.403 --> 00:26:47.872
was Merkel in Germany.

00:26:47.872 --> 00:26:48.840
I mean, think about it.

00:26:48.840 --> 00:26:50.075
- [Rubin] Yeah.

00:26:50.075 --> 00:26:51.743 align:start size:84% position:16%
- 32 years ago, a
million college kids

00:26:51.743 --> 00:26:55.313
went to Tiananmen Square for
the idea of freedom of speech.

00:26:55.313 --> 00:26:57.582
The 3 million people out
of 8 million in Hong Kong

00:26:57.582 --> 00:27:00.619 align:start size:97% position:3%
that would go out in the rain
craved freedom of speech,

00:27:00.619 --> 00:27:04.289
and we had just allowed that
to be denied to an American.

00:27:04.289 --> 00:27:05.957 align:start size:81% position:19%
And then once they
did it to him,

00:27:05.957 --> 00:27:09.294 align:start size:97% position:3%
they did it to everybody else
that they disagreed with.

00:27:09.294 --> 00:27:13.231 align:start size:97% position:3%
Why have we come so far that
you and I could sit down,

00:27:13.231 --> 00:27:15.200
you could have a total
different philosophy?

00:27:15.200 --> 00:27:19.137
We could debate, but we don't
have to dislike each other.

00:27:19.137 --> 00:27:21.606
When we take away the debate,

00:27:21.606 --> 00:27:24.242 align:start size:94% position:6%
we're taking away a
foundation of who we are.

00:27:24.242 --> 00:27:25.443
I mean, I should not be afraid

00:27:25.443 --> 00:27:26.878 align:start size:88% position:13%
that someone has a
different belief than I

00:27:26.878 --> 00:27:28.279
and debate the idea.

00:27:28.279 --> 00:27:30.849 align:start position:0%
It either makes my ideas
stronger, makes me believe more.

00:27:30.849 --> 00:27:34.352
I mean, I'm a Republican, but
my family are all Democrats.

00:27:34.352 --> 00:27:35.787
I chose to be in this party.

00:27:35.787 --> 00:27:37.288 align:start size:84% position:16%
So when someone
wants to challenge me

00:27:37.288 --> 00:27:38.823
on my conservative beliefs,

00:27:38.823 --> 00:27:41.092
I wasn't born here,
I chose to come here.

00:27:41.092 --> 00:27:42.727 align:start size:84% position:16%
I think I'm gonna have
a stronger belief.

00:27:42.727 --> 00:27:45.230 align:start size:84% position:16%
I rejected what
somebody said at home

00:27:45.230 --> 00:27:47.098 align:start size:84% position:16%
and was seeking
out something else.

00:27:47.098 --> 00:27:48.633 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Wait, let's stick
with that for a moment,

00:27:48.633 --> 00:27:50.268
because I think you know a
little bit about my story.

00:27:50.268 --> 00:27:51.102
- Yeah.

00:27:51.102 --> 00:27:52.303
- I was a big Lefty.

00:27:52.303 --> 00:27:53.838 align:start size:88% position:13%
So I'm curious, I didn't
know that about you.

00:27:53.838 --> 00:27:55.807
So you come from a
family of Democrats.

00:27:55.807 --> 00:27:57.242
- We were all Dems, I was the
first Republican in my family.

00:27:57.242 --> 00:27:59.978
- What was your moment?

00:27:59.978 --> 00:28:01.079
- So I'm older than you.

00:28:01.079 --> 00:28:01.646 align:start size:84% position:16%
- We'll get back to-
- I'm 56.

00:28:01.646 --> 00:28:03.214
- Yeah.

00:28:03.214 --> 00:28:06.151 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Reagan was the first
president I could vote for.

00:28:06.151 --> 00:28:07.652
It was in the second term.

00:28:07.652 --> 00:28:10.255
But when I was in elementary
school, I watched junior high,

00:28:10.255 --> 00:28:13.692
Jimmy Carter, go on
TV, put a sweater on,

00:28:15.126 --> 00:28:16.661
tell me to turn the heater down

00:28:16.661 --> 00:28:19.431
and told me the best days
of America were behind us.

00:28:19.431 --> 00:28:21.633 align:start size:88% position:13%
- Yeah.
- He accept mediocracy.

00:28:21.633 --> 00:28:24.335
And I watch this other guy
go to a podium and says,

00:28:24.335 --> 00:28:25.870
"No pastels.

00:28:25.870 --> 00:28:29.207
Fly the bold colors and go to
that shiny city on the hill."

00:28:29.207 --> 00:28:31.042
I knew what he meant, right?

00:28:31.042 --> 00:28:34.045 align:start size:84% position:16%
That America is
exceptional, right?

00:28:35.280 --> 00:28:36.514
If you come to my office,

00:28:36.514 --> 00:28:37.315
I want you to come
to DC sometimes.

00:28:37.315 --> 00:28:38.750
- [Rubin] Yeah. Yeah.

00:28:38.750 --> 00:28:40.051 align:start size:94% position:6%
- So I have this portrait
of Reagan behind me,

00:28:40.051 --> 00:28:41.619
it's in color, right?

00:28:41.619 --> 00:28:44.189 align:start size:88% position:13%
And I have this other
portrait that's very big

00:28:44.189 --> 00:28:45.590
and it's Lincoln.

00:28:45.590 --> 00:28:46.925
And it's in black and white,

00:28:46.925 --> 00:28:49.794
The greatest challenge
ever to our constitution

00:28:49.794 --> 00:28:51.362
was the Civil War.

00:28:51.362 --> 00:28:53.865
And at night, if you sat
back in what's happening

00:28:53.865 --> 00:28:57.368 align:start size:97% position:3%
and you ask these two people
to give us advice today,

00:28:57.368 --> 00:28:58.703
Lincoln would say,

00:28:58.703 --> 00:29:00.839 align:start size:94% position:6%
believe in the
exceptionalism of America.

00:29:00.839 --> 00:29:03.641
And this is what makes me mad
coming off the 4th of July.

00:29:03.641 --> 00:29:07.245 align:start size:84% position:16%
Why would we celebrate
the progress?

00:29:07.245 --> 00:29:09.981
We're not perfect, but as we
strive from a perfect union,

00:29:09.981 --> 00:29:13.651 align:start size:84% position:16%
Lincoln said,
"Conceived in Liberty

00:29:13.651 --> 00:29:16.454 align:start size:97% position:3%
and dedicate the proposition
that we're all equal."

00:29:16.454 --> 00:29:19.324
He goes on to say, "But if we
fail government of the people,

00:29:19.324 --> 00:29:23.461
by the people, for the people
shall not perish from earth."

00:29:23.461 --> 00:29:24.429
We weren't the world power,

00:29:24.429 --> 00:29:27.332
but he understood the strength

00:29:27.332 --> 00:29:30.568
of what a democracy and
a republic were, right?

00:29:30.568 --> 00:29:34.939 align:start size:97% position:3%
There is no other nation in
the world conceived in Liberty

00:29:34.939 --> 00:29:36.775
and dedicated they're all equal.

00:29:36.775 --> 00:29:39.611 align:start size:84% position:16%
Maybe Israel, but
no other one, right?

00:29:39.611 --> 00:29:42.147
Lincoln would also
tell us this, right?

00:29:42.147 --> 00:29:44.749
"As an elected official,

00:29:44.749 --> 00:29:47.385
don't be afraid of making
a tough decision," right?

00:29:47.385 --> 00:29:50.955 align:start size:97% position:3%
The whole debate of slavery
didn't start in 1850.

00:29:50.955 --> 00:29:52.891 align:start size:84% position:16%
It was the creation
of our country,

00:29:52.891 --> 00:29:56.628 align:start size:97% position:3%
but they thought it was too
divided, to divisional, right?

00:29:56.628 --> 00:29:58.963 align:start size:88% position:13%
That it would divide
us, divide the colonies.

00:29:58.963 --> 00:30:00.565
So they said to put it aside,

00:30:00.565 --> 00:30:04.202 align:start size:84% position:16%
and doing so, hundreds
of thousands of
their children died.

00:30:04.202 --> 00:30:06.304 align:start size:88% position:13%
We don't have a decision
like that today,

00:30:06.304 --> 00:30:09.707 align:start size:97% position:3%
but our debt, our challenge
with China, right?

00:30:09.707 --> 00:30:11.276
Are we too afraid
as elected official

00:30:11.276 --> 00:30:13.812 align:start size:84% position:16%
to make a decision
that we can lose upon?

00:30:13.812 --> 00:30:15.914
But what happens
is you narrow it,

00:30:15.914 --> 00:30:17.115 align:start size:84% position:16%
you're eventually
gonna make decisions,

00:30:17.115 --> 00:30:19.017
but you're gonna
have fewer options.

00:30:19.017 --> 00:30:21.986 align:start size:97% position:3%
And the other thing Lincoln
would tell us today is,

00:30:21.986 --> 00:30:24.088 align:start size:88% position:13%
don't blame other people
for your problems.

00:30:24.088 --> 00:30:27.125
Think about it, he gets
elected November, 1860,

00:30:27.125 --> 00:30:30.495
but not sworn until March, 1861.

00:30:30.495 --> 00:30:33.264
Never once did he
blamed Buchanan.

00:30:33.264 --> 00:30:34.933
He understood where he was.

00:30:34.933 --> 00:30:36.467
What was it, seven
states left the union

00:30:36.467 --> 00:30:38.436
in that short amount of time?

00:30:38.436 --> 00:30:40.839 align:start size:81% position:19%
Now, Reagan, I
have him in color.

00:30:40.839 --> 00:30:42.974
And this is something for you,

00:30:42.974 --> 00:30:44.742
this is something for
every conservative.

00:30:44.742 --> 00:30:46.044
This is what he'd be,

00:30:46.044 --> 00:30:48.313
and he'd even say it
to liberals, right?

00:30:48.313 --> 00:30:50.481
If you believe in
your philosophy,

00:30:50.481 --> 00:30:53.351
if you truly believe your
philosophy and principles

00:30:53.351 --> 00:30:57.255
bring people more freedom,
don't be angry, be happy.

00:30:57.255 --> 00:31:00.191 align:start size:97% position:3%
I get upset that these people
say I'm more conservative

00:31:00.191 --> 00:31:01.693
'cause I'm angry.

00:31:01.693 --> 00:31:03.895
All that means is less people
wanna have dinner with you.

00:31:03.895 --> 00:31:05.096
- [Rubin] Yeah.

00:31:05.096 --> 00:31:06.664 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Reagan brought
Democrats in, right?

00:31:06.664 --> 00:31:08.433 align:start size:84% position:16%
People wanna know
what he was drinking.

00:31:08.433 --> 00:31:09.968
Why was he so happy?

00:31:09.968 --> 00:31:11.703
He had the 11th commandment.

00:31:11.703 --> 00:31:14.405 align:start size:84% position:16%
He didn't tear another
Republican down.

00:31:14.405 --> 00:31:15.573
He brought humor to it,

00:31:15.573 --> 00:31:16.841 align:start size:81% position:19%
but he knew-
- He was basically

00:31:16.841 --> 00:31:17.775
in the "Rat Pack."

00:31:17.775 --> 00:31:18.943
The guy was cool.

00:31:18.943 --> 00:31:20.111 align:start size:88% position:13%
I just watched the
"Rat Pack" documentary.

00:31:20.111 --> 00:31:21.179 align:start size:88% position:13%
He's like, he's hanging
out with Sinatra.

00:31:21.179 --> 00:31:22.113
Like, yeah.

00:31:22.113 --> 00:31:22.847
- You want it to be around.

00:31:22.847 --> 00:31:24.048
- Yeah.

00:31:24.048 --> 00:31:25.550 align:start size:84% position:16%
- The other thing
Reagan would tell us,

00:31:25.550 --> 00:31:27.051 align:start size:94% position:6%
and this would be critical
for the time and nature

00:31:27.051 --> 00:31:31.122 align:start size:97% position:3%
of who we are, peace without
freedom is meaningless.

00:31:31.122 --> 00:31:32.290
Think about that.

00:31:32.290 --> 00:31:34.492 align:start size:84% position:16%
Peace without freedom
is meaningless.

00:31:34.492 --> 00:31:37.028
It is human nature
that we crave peace,

00:31:37.028 --> 00:31:41.633 align:start size:97% position:3%
but we cannot attain it
without having freedom, okay?

00:31:41.633 --> 00:31:43.034
So, so many times in history

00:31:43.034 --> 00:31:45.103
we watched if people
has failed, right?

00:31:45.103 --> 00:31:47.238
Chamberlain, peace for our time.

00:31:47.238 --> 00:31:48.740
What'd he do?

00:31:48.740 --> 00:31:50.708 align:start size:94% position:6%
He claimed he created
peace by appeasing Hitler,

00:31:50.708 --> 00:31:52.110
by giving him a country.

00:31:52.110 --> 00:31:53.678 align:start size:84% position:16%
All Hitler understood,
your weakness

00:31:53.678 --> 00:31:56.681
and I'm gonna beat you, right?

00:31:56.681 --> 00:31:59.584 align:start size:84% position:16%
Barack Obama,
concerned about Iran.

00:32:00.985 --> 00:32:03.354 align:start size:97% position:3%
He gave them billions of
dollars thinking they would,

00:32:03.354 --> 00:32:05.223
and then said, "You can
have a nuclear weapon.

00:32:05.223 --> 00:32:07.125
You just have to wait 10 years."

00:32:07.125 --> 00:32:09.394 align:start size:97% position:3%
There was no freedom in
that, so there was no peace.

00:32:09.394 --> 00:32:11.763
So they took that money
and they funded terrorism

00:32:11.763 --> 00:32:13.097
around the Gulf.

00:32:14.499 --> 00:32:15.867
Reagan had the same dilemma.

00:32:15.867 --> 00:32:18.169 line:0%
He's in his second term.

00:32:18.169 --> 00:32:21.072 align:start position:0% line:0%
He's sitting next to Gorbachev,
he's in Iceland, right?

00:32:21.072 --> 00:32:22.440 line:0%
He's actually having
no staff in the room

00:32:22.440 --> 00:32:25.576 line:0%
and he's negotiating
a nuclear reduction.

00:32:25.576 --> 00:32:28.246 align:start size:88% position:13%
He's getting almost
everything he asked for,

00:32:28.246 --> 00:32:30.214 align:start size:84% position:16%
but Gorbachev
asked him for this.

00:32:30.214 --> 00:32:34.085
Gorbachev asked him to
end the SDI program.

00:32:34.085 --> 00:32:36.487 align:start size:97% position:3%
But remember the time and
place when Gorbachev has him.

00:32:36.487 --> 00:32:37.455
It was a joke, right?

00:32:37.455 --> 00:32:40.391
People called it "Star Wars."

00:32:40.391 --> 00:32:42.760
It is the Iron Dome today
that shoots missiles down.

00:32:42.760 --> 00:32:44.329
It's what protects us.

00:32:44.329 --> 00:32:46.731
Reagan, you know what he does?

00:32:46.731 --> 00:32:48.333
He doesn't say, "Let
me talk to Congress."

00:32:48.333 --> 00:32:50.535 align:start size:84% position:16%
He says, "No, but I'll
do something else.

00:32:50.535 --> 00:32:53.771
I'll share it with you so
the world will be safe."

00:32:53.771 --> 00:32:55.406
Gorbachev declines.

00:32:55.406 --> 00:32:58.042
So Reagan has, here's a document

00:32:59.210 --> 00:33:02.547
that he got a lot of
what he was asking for,

00:33:02.547 --> 00:33:04.449
but it didn't do anything
for those in the gulags.

00:33:04.449 --> 00:33:06.784 align:start size:84% position:16%
It didn't do anything
for (indistinct)

00:33:06.784 --> 00:33:08.720
So he realized there
was no freedom in it,

00:33:08.720 --> 00:33:10.254
so there would be no peace.

00:33:10.254 --> 00:33:11.756
So he got up and he walked away.

00:33:11.756 --> 00:33:13.992 align:start position:0%
And there's this famous picture
of him like in a raincoat,

00:33:13.992 --> 00:33:14.826
going out rejected.

00:33:14.826 --> 00:33:16.260
- [Rubin] Yeah. Yeah.

00:33:16.260 --> 00:33:19.097 align:start size:97% position:3%
- Every media, elite media,
criticized him, right?

00:33:19.097 --> 00:33:21.833
But had not walked
away at that moment,

00:33:21.833 --> 00:33:23.468
the Berlin Wall would
not have collapsed,

00:33:23.468 --> 00:33:26.037 align:start size:84% position:16%
the Soviet Union would
not have collapsed.

00:33:26.037 --> 00:33:28.673
And what's interesting
to me in modern time,

00:33:28.673 --> 00:33:30.475
there've been two presidents

00:33:30.475 --> 00:33:32.210
that've gotten the
Nobel Peace Prize,

00:33:32.210 --> 00:33:34.479
Jimmy Carter and Barack Obama.

00:33:34.479 --> 00:33:36.981 align:start size:84% position:16%
The two presidents who
deserved it really,

00:33:36.981 --> 00:33:38.916
were Ronald Reagan
and Donald Trump.

00:33:38.916 --> 00:33:42.253 align:start size:94% position:6%
What he did in the Abraham
Accords were amazing.

00:33:42.253 --> 00:33:43.488 align:start size:97% position:3%
But-
- I thought we're not allowed

00:33:43.488 --> 00:33:44.756
to call them that
anymore, right?

00:33:44.756 --> 00:33:46.024
Isn't that what the
administration says?

00:33:46.024 --> 00:33:48.426
They're the Middle East
peace, not really thing

00:33:48.426 --> 00:33:49.627
or something like that.

00:33:49.627 --> 00:33:50.161 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Well, they're trying
to tear them down.

00:33:50.161 --> 00:33:51.396
- Yeah.

00:33:51.396 --> 00:33:53.431
- But this is the challenge.

00:33:53.431 --> 00:33:56.701
When you think about that,
what drove me to this party.

00:33:56.701 --> 00:33:57.969
And I love...

00:33:57.969 --> 00:33:59.837 align:start size:84% position:16%
We had a debate the
other day on the floor

00:33:59.837 --> 00:34:01.339
to remove statues, right?

00:34:01.339 --> 00:34:03.574
Every state gets
us into statues.

00:34:03.574 --> 00:34:05.877
So the Democrats had a bill
to remove these statutes

00:34:05.877 --> 00:34:08.112 align:start size:88% position:13%
as these were
Confederate individuals.

00:34:08.112 --> 00:34:09.247
So I go to the floor.

00:34:09.247 --> 00:34:10.181
I voted for the bill.

00:34:10.181 --> 00:34:11.449
I voted for it last time.

00:34:11.449 --> 00:34:13.351
And so let me just
get this straight.

00:34:13.351 --> 00:34:16.120
You wanna remove
Democrat statues

00:34:17.989 --> 00:34:20.158
that were sent to us by states

00:34:20.158 --> 00:34:22.627
that were controlled
by Democrats years ago,

00:34:22.627 --> 00:34:24.929
and sent to Congress that
had a Democrat majority

00:34:24.929 --> 00:34:26.864
that accepted them.

00:34:26.864 --> 00:34:28.166
Well, I just didn't think
that bill went far enough?

00:34:28.166 --> 00:34:30.168 align:start size:81% position:19%
Why didn't they
change their name?

00:34:30.168 --> 00:34:31.402
Do you realize.

00:34:32.603 --> 00:34:33.738
- Is that where this all ends?

00:34:33.738 --> 00:34:34.972
It ends with the
Democratic Party

00:34:34.972 --> 00:34:36.874
basically having to
commit Harry Carey

00:34:36.874 --> 00:34:39.710 align:start size:94% position:6%
because it was pretty much
them that did all this.

00:34:39.710 --> 00:34:41.479 align:start size:84% position:16%
- Who's the first
Republican president?

00:34:41.479 --> 00:34:42.480
- Abraham Lincoln.

00:34:42.480 --> 00:34:43.314 align:start size:75% position:25%
- That's right.
- Yeah.

00:34:43.314 --> 00:34:44.549
'Cause he left.

00:34:44.549 --> 00:34:46.150
He didn't wanna be
pulled out the wigs

00:34:46.150 --> 00:34:48.486
and then they brought the
non slavery Democrats.

00:34:48.486 --> 00:34:49.320
Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:34:49.320 --> 00:34:50.254
And then what happened?

00:34:50.254 --> 00:34:51.522
Have you ever thought from,

00:34:51.522 --> 00:34:52.523
and if you're gonna
write another book,

00:34:52.523 --> 00:34:53.357
we should collaborate on this.

00:34:53.357 --> 00:34:54.325
- Yeah.

00:34:54.325 --> 00:34:56.094
- What would America look like

00:34:56.094 --> 00:34:58.563 align:start size:81% position:19%
had Lincoln not
been assassinated?

00:34:58.563 --> 00:35:00.431
Malice towards none.

00:35:00.431 --> 00:35:02.166
Would we ever had Jim Crow laws?

00:35:02.166 --> 00:35:03.301 align:start size:81% position:19%
Do you know how
Jim Crow laws came

00:35:03.301 --> 00:35:05.236
and who authored Jim Crow laws?

00:35:05.236 --> 00:35:06.671 align:start size:72% position:28%
The Democrats.
- Yeah.

00:35:06.671 --> 00:35:07.605
- How did they do it?

00:35:07.605 --> 00:35:09.674
Because after the Civil War,

00:35:09.674 --> 00:35:12.810 align:start size:88% position:13%
the south was controlled
by Republicans,

00:35:12.810 --> 00:35:14.679
white Republicans and
black Republicans.

00:35:14.679 --> 00:35:16.881
And who'd they elect,
